    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. To be more specific, I'm dealing with cases where the lesions have a very strange, almost \"unnatural\" appearance—like deeply recessed, jagged-edged patches or strange, crusty textures that don't follow typical dermatological patterns. When there's such a significant deviation from the norm, is there a specific hierarchy for using \"other specified\" codes (like the L98.8 series) versus just sticking to the \"unspecified\" L98.9? I want to make sure we aren't being too vague, but the more specific we try to get, the more the morphology seems to defy standard classification.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dealing with highly atypical dermatological presentations requires a nuanced approach in coding. You can still use the L98.9 as your primary general code since it serves as a catch-all category for disorders due to the skin and subcutaneous tissue when a specific condition isn't clearly diagnosable. However, in this case, given the unusual morphology described, it would be prudent to append additional codes that specifically reference crusty textures (R23.0), or deep depressions in the skin (L98.4), if available. It is crucial to gather comprehensive clinical data, possibly including biopsies and skin smears, and correlate them with the presented symptoms. Record these observations diligently and, if the situation allows, consult with a specialist who can offer a more precise diagnosis. In eventual code sequences, always err on the side of specificity, followed by the default general code, and be prepared to use the Rationale for Modification (RFM) field to explain why a particular code doesn't fit well.\n\nAdditionally, you can use \"other specified,\" such as L98.89, during your evaluation if there is a closer match for a known condition that partially fits, even if the morphed condition presents some deviations. Always back up your chosen code",
      "source": "target",
